    {
      h2: 'Where the number comes from',
      paras: [
        "The heart rate comes from the sensor on the back of the Watch. An iPhone has no built-in heart rate sensor. Refresher reads your heart rate only from an Apple Watch, through Apple Health.",
        "Apple's own tips for a clean reading are short. Keep your wrist and the Watch clean and dry, because water and sweat can cause a poor recording, and keep the back of the Watch against your skin. Sitting still helps too, which suits a breathing session anyway.",
      ],
    },
    {
      h2: 'One reading after, or one before and one after',
      paras: [
        "A single number on a Summary screen tells you where your heart rate was when you finished. It doesn't tell you where it started, so you can't see a change. If you want to know whether five minutes of slow breathing moved your pulse today, you need a reading from the start as well.",
        "Even with two readings, a short session is hard to read. On a short session the first and last readings are often the same, and a wrist sensor reading only a few samples can produce a difference that's just noise.",
      ],
      aside: [
        "In Refresher I made the completion card show the change only when there's a reading at both ends and at least three samples behind each. Without them it leaves the row out instead of printing a number built on noise. It reports a drop, no change or a rise, because no change is a common result on a short session and I didn't want to hide it.",
      ],
    },
    {
      h2: 'Heart rate variability is a different question',
      paras: [
        "If what you're after is heart rate variability (HRV), the sources disagree on whether a Breathe session records it. Sano Labs writes that Apple Watch measures HRV overnight and \"not during mindfulness sessions\". Athlytic's help centre says a Mindfulness session triggers an HRV reading, warns that HRV taken during one is usually higher than your sleep average, and advises breathing naturally if you want a reading you can compare.",
        "Either way, Apple Health stores HRV as SDNN, and from what I've seen building Refresher, a Watch writes a handful of those samples a day, not a stream. Nobody can draw an honest before-and-after HRV line through one five-minute session from that. Read HRV over weeks.",
      ],
      aside: [
        "I refused to put a before-and-after HRV number on Refresher's completion screen for exactly that reason. A figure that can't be produced honestly is worse than no figure, and the person who wants HRV is the person who'd catch it. You see your HRV alongside the days you practise instead, as a 30-day trend.",
      ],
    },
    {
      h2: 'How Refresher shows it',
      paras: [
        "Refresher is a breathing pacer for iPhone and iPad, with a companion Apple Watch app. With an Apple Watch on and heart rate tracking switched on in Settings, it keeps a heart rate reading from the start of the session and one from the end, and the completion card shows the change. Tracking is a separate switch from the Apple Health permission and starts off.",
        "The same card shows your latest HRV reading from Apple Health with its date, never a session delta. In Statistics, a 30-day HRV chart puts your Apple Health readings next to the days you practise. It doesn't appear until there are two days of data, and it's free through your tenth qualifying session, then part of Premium, which has a 7-day free trial. The heart rate change and the latest HRV reading on the completion card are free.",
        "For the session itself, Coherent paces 5.5 seconds in and 5.5 out, about 5.5 breaths a minute, and it's free from the first session.",
      ],
